# Single-row updates (API v1 addition)

Status: proposed. Drafted while the owner was away. Decisions I made on my own are listed under [Assumptions and calls made](#assumptions-and-calls-made).

## Problem

Partners can only change a price list by `PUT`ting the whole document with `If-Match`. Several partners and our own staff often edit the same list in the same hour, so a partner that wants to change one price has to re-read the document, apply the change, and `PUT` again. Every unrelated edit by someone else in between turns that into a `412`. Partners want to change one row without sending the whole document.

## Summary

Add a row sub-resource to v1. This is an additive change, and nothing already in v1 changes.

```text
GET   /v1/price-lists/{id}/rows/{rowId}   -> one row + row ETag
PATCH /v1/price-lists/{id}/rows/{rowId}   -> change fields of one row, If-Match: <row ETag> required
```

The concurrency rule is the same one partners already follow for the document: **read, send back the ETag you read in `If-Match`, and get `412` if it is stale.** The only difference is scope. The ETag covers one row, not the whole document, so edits to other rows no longer cause conflicts.

## Row identity

- Every row has a stable, server-assigned `id` (12-character Base58, the same format as our other entity IDs). It never changes when the row is edited or reordered, or when other rows are added or removed.
- Rows are addressed by `id` and never by array index. An index shifts whenever someone else inserts or deletes a row, so a `PATCH` sent to an index could silently land on the wrong product.
- `id` appears on each row in the `GET /v1/price-lists/{id}` document. This adds a field to v1 responses.
- On `PUT`, a row that includes an `id` the list already has is the same row. A row with no `id` is a new row and gets one assigned. Partners that ignore `id` and send rows without it keep working as before (see assumption A1).

## `GET /v1/price-lists/{id}/rows/{rowId}`

- `200` returns the row in the same schema it has inside the document (one normalized row schema everywhere), with an `ETag` header for that row.
- `404` if the list or the row doesn't exist or isn't visible to the caller. The error code tells them apart: `price_list_not_found` or `row_not_found`.
- Uses the same authorization as `GET /v1/price-lists/{id}`.

## `PATCH /v1/price-lists/{id}/rows/{rowId}`

Request:

```http
PATCH /v1/price-lists/pL7k2mQx9aBc/rows/R4tY8uNw3zKd
If-Match: "r-8f3a..."
Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json

{ "unitPrice": "12.40", "currency": "EUR" }
```

- **Body:** JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396) applied to the row object. Fields that are omitted stay unchanged. `id` can't be changed, and a body that tries is rejected with `422 row_id_immutable`. Setting a field to `null` removes it only if the row schema allows that field to be absent. Otherwise it is a `422`.
- **`If-Match` is required** and takes the row ETag from either `GET .../rows/{rowId}` or an earlier `PATCH` response.
- **Validation:** the patched row goes through the same validation and product rules as a row in a `PUT`. Both endpoints call one shared "update price-list row" capability, and the browser app uses that capability too. The rules are not duplicated in the HTTP adapter.
- **Authorization:** the same permission as `PUT /v1/price-lists/{id}`. It is enforced once, at the product boundary.

Responses:

| Status | Code | Meaning / next step |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `200` | | Change landed. Body is the full updated row. `ETag` is the new **row** ETag. |
| `412` | `row_changed` | Someone changed this row since your read. Re-`GET` the row, reapply, retry. |
| `428` | `if_match_required` | `If-Match` missing. `GET` the row first. |
| `404` | `price_list_not_found` / `row_not_found` | Wrong ID, row deleted, or no access. |
| `415` | `unsupported_media_type` | Use `application/merge-patch+json`. |
| `422` | `row_invalid` / `row_id_immutable` | The patched row fails validation. The error names the field and the rule, without echoing the whole body. |

Errors use the existing v1 typed error shape (assumption A4).

### What `PATCH` deliberately does not return

The `PATCH` response does **not** include the document's ETag. A successful row patch changes the document, so the document ETag changes. If we gave the caller the new document ETag, a partner holding an older full copy could `PUT` it with that fresh ETag and silently overwrite every other row that changed in the meantime. A caller gets a document ETag only from a complete `GET` of the document, or from its own `PUT` that was stored exactly as sent. That is the same as today.

As a result, a partner that mixes styles (`GET` document, `PATCH` a row, then `PUT` the old document) will get `412` on the `PUT`. That is intended: their copy of the document really is stale.

## Retries and unattended sync

- `PATCH` with `If-Match` is safe to retry. Suppose a request times out after it already landed. The retry then gets `412 row_changed`, because the ETag moved. The partner re-`GET`s the row and sees whether their values are already there. No idempotency key is needed: the precondition already prevents double application, and a merge patch reapplied to the same values does nothing anyway.
- Unattended syncs that every few minutes push "the price for row X is Y" can do `GET` row, then `PATCH`. That costs two small requests in place of a full download and upload of a few hundred rows, and conflicts only happen when someone else touched that same row.

## Unchanged

- `GET /v1/price-lists/{id}` and `PUT /v1/price-lists/{id}` keep their published behavior: whole-document `If-Match`, and `412` on any stale ETag. The only change is the additive row `id` field.
- No v2. Everything here is additive, so it stays in v1.

## Rollout

1. Backfill `id` on every existing row.
2. Publish the row schema change and the two new operations in `https://api.ledgerline.example/openapi.json`, and agree on that contract before implementing.
3. Implement the shared row-update capability, then the API adapter. Move the browser app's row editing onto the same capability.
4. Bump the API's minor version, and update the changelog and partner docs in the same change.

## Assumptions and calls made

- **A1. Rows have no stable ID today.** The brief doesn't say either way. If they already do, use the existing one and skip the backfill. Assumption A1 also covers `PUT`: a client that sends rows without `id` replaces the document as before, and every row in it gets a fresh `id`. Any `rowId` a caller held for that list becomes `404`. That's acceptable because `PUT` is a full replace. **Owner should confirm.**
- **A2. Concurrency follows the existing contract (required `If-Match`, `412` when stale) at row granularity.** I didn't make `If-Match` optional with last-write-wins. Partners and staff are known to edit the same lists in the same hour, and without a validator the server can't tell what the caller last saw. Requiring it also matches what partners already do for `PUT`.
- **A3. Scope is update only.** Adding and deleting single rows are separate operations with their own destructive boundaries (`POST .../rows` and `DELETE .../rows/{rowId}`). I left them out until partners ask. Batch row updates are also left out. If they come, each row should get its own result, in input order.
- **A4. An existing typed v1 error body exists.** If it doesn't, the codes in the table above become the start of one.
- **A5. Merge patch over JSON Patch.** Rows are flat objects of a few fields, and merge patch is simpler for partners to produce. If rows contain nested arrays that partners need to edit element by element, revisit this.
- **A6. Field names in the example** (`unitPrice`, `currency`) are illustrative. The brief doesn't define the row schema.
- **Not decided, needs owner:** whether `PUT` should later stop returning `412` for edits to rows the caller didn't touch, and merge per row instead. That would change published v1 behavior partners have relied on for two years, so this design doesn't touch it.
